Yes — and it's worse than the report suggested. I probed the actual failure modes with the real dependencies rather than reading the code:

```
no header  | negotiator: ["*"]      | THROWS RangeError: invalid language tag: *
"*"        | negotiator: ["*"]      | THROWS RangeError: invalid language tag: *
"en_US"    | negotiator: ["en_US"]  | THROWS RangeError: invalid language tag: en_US
""         | negotiator: []         | → en   (fine)
"en-US,en" | negotiator: ["en-US"]  | → en   (fine)
```

Three distinct triggers, not one. `packages/internationalization/middleware.ts:18` passes `negotiator.languages()` straight into `matchLocale`, and Negotiator returns the wildcard `["*"]` when there's no `Accept-Language` at all — while `@formatjs/intl-localematcher` validates through `Intl.getCanonicalLocales` and throws on anything non-canonical. A malformed-but-plausible tag like `en_US` (underscore instead of hyphen) throws too. The `defaultLocale: "en"` third argument never gets a chance to help, because the throw happens during validation, not matching.

**Why this matters today, not abstractly.** Real browsers always send `Accept-Language`, so your audience is fine. But `apps/web/proxy.ts:38` deliberately allows `CATEGORY:PREVIEW` through Arcjet "to show OG images" — and link unfurlers are exactly the class of client that sends a minimal request. The moment you paste `hastoggle.dev/live` into Slack, X, or LinkedIn, an unfurler that omits the header gets a 500 instead of your OG card. Same for `CATEGORY:MONITOR` uptime checks and any `curl` you or anyone else runs. Every one of those is also a Sentry event.

The guard is small, and filtering beats a bare `try`/`catch` because it degrades gracefully instead of collapsing to the default:

```ts
/** Negotiator yields "*" when a client sends no Accept-Language (curl, uptime
 *  monitors, link unfurlers), and passes malformed tags like "en_US" through
 *  verbatim. @formatjs/intl-localematcher validates via Intl.getCanonicalLocales
 *  and throws RangeError on both, which surfaces as a 500 on every route. */
const canonicalizableTags = (tags: string[]) =>
  tags.filter((tag) => {
    try {
      Intl.getCanonicalLocales(tag);
      return true;
    } catch {
      return false;
    }
  });
```

Verified against all eight cases: `*` → `en`, `en_US` → `en`, `ja-JP` → `en`, `de-DE` → `de`, and usefully `"en_US, de-DE"` → **`de`** — a blanket `catch` would have thrown that request's real preference away and served English.

This is genuinely separate from the address strip, so it wants its own commit and a test — `packages/internationalization` has no test file today, and unlike the strip this *is* pure logic worth pinning down (the `["*"]` case especially, since it's the one no browser will ever reproduce for you manually).

**Do you want it on this branch as a third commit, or on its own branch and PR?** I'd put it on its own, so the masterclass URL change stays a clean single-purpose PR — but if you want it live before you share the `/live` link anywhere, riding along on this branch is faster.

Meanwhile: both tasks passed review, the working tree is clean (I reverted 178 files of unrelated `bun run fix` churn the implementer left behind — never committed), and the final whole-branch review is running in the background on Opus. Two commits: `eae126d` the redirect, `ce98e50` the strip.

